= The Democratic Party Challenges Erim

As the motion of interpellation comes before Parliament, the votes of Ecevit's supporters in the CHP, the MGP, and a number of AP deputies join those of the DP to pass it. What was expected to be another unsuccessful challenge to Erim's government instead becomes a humiliating parliamentary defeat, shocking the political establishment in Ankara. Even deputies from the AP, which had largely supported the military's efforts to erode the 1961 Constitution, have defied their party chairman, Süleyman Demirel. With his authority so openly rejected by Parliament, Erim sees no reason to remain in office and submits his resignation.

However, our unexpected parliamentary victory comes at a considerable political cost. Upon learning that Ecevit has formed an informal alliance with three other parties without consulting the General Chairman, İnönü is furious. In his view, Ecevit's actions have undermined the party leadership's attempts to preserve Parliament while avoiding an open confrontation with the armed forces. İnönü increasingly regards Ecevit as reckless and dismissive of the authority of the CHP leadership. Their disagreement over how best to defend parliamentary democracy, and what that duty demands of Kemalism, is becoming a bitter personal conflict. What İnönü considers Ecevit's unruly and disrespectful conduct is now as important to him as the political dispute itself.

